  {
    "text": "Translating long-term memory into embodied plans is a method for addressing partial observability, but its implementation involves distinct technical problems (e.g., memory retrieval, plan grounding) that are not fully solved.",
    "topic": "Nature of the challenge"
  },
  {
    "text": "The WorldLines benchmark measures the ability to translate long-term memory into plans as a core capability, indicating that it is treated as an independent axis of evaluation, not merely a sub-problem of partial observability.",
    "topic": "Benchmark framing"
  },
  {
    "text": "Partial observability refers to the lack of complete state information, while memory-to-plan translation refers to the process of using stored episodic or semantic knowledge to generate actions; one is a problem condition, the other a candidate solution procedure.",
    "topic": "Conceptual distinction"
  },
  {
    "text": "Overwritten world states can make long-term memory stale or irrelevant, so memory-to-plan translation faces the additional challenge of detecting and handling outdated memories, which is orthogonal to the original partial observability.",
    "topic": "Memory integrity challenge"
  }
]
